on of non-sunny lines that are not vertical? Non-sunny lines are horizontal or slope -1. Horizontal line y=1 would cover (n-2,1) only. Horizontal line y=2 would cover (n-2,2) only. Horizontal line y=3 would cover (n-2,3) only. So we would need three horizontal lines to cover all three points, but we have only n-2 non-sunny lines total, but we could allocate them. However, we also need to cover other columns. The claim that \"must be vertical\" is based on the fact that we have limited number of non-sunny lines, but they didn't explicitly argue why we cannot use horizontal or slope -1 lines to cover column n-2. Let's examine: If we use a slope -1 line, it can cover at most one point per column, but could cover two points in column n-2? No, slope -1 line is not vertical, so it cannot have two points with same x-coordinate. So any non-vertical line can intersect column n-2 at most once. So to cover three points in column n-2, we need at least three non-sunny lines (vertical/horizontal/slope -1). But we have only n-2 non-sunny lines total, but we could allocate three of them to column n-2. However, we also need to cover other columns. The solution's argument is that we have only n-2 non-sunny lines total, but we need to allocate at least one vertical line to column n-2 because we need to cover the three points, and we cannot allocate three non-vertical lines because we need to cover other columns as well. But is that rigorous? Let's examine more carefully.\n\nWe have n lines total, k=2 sunny lines, so n-2 non-sunny lines. We need to cover all points. Column n-2 has 3 points. Each sunny line can cover at most one point in that column. So the three points require at least 3 - k = 1 non-sunny line covering at least one of them. Actually we need to cover 3 points; we have 2 sunny lines, each can cover at most one point, so they can cover at most 2 points in column n-2. So at least one point in column n-2 must be covered by a non-sunny line. But does that non-sunny line have