ts (a,b) with a+b ≤ n+1.\n\nWe need to see if any arrangement with exactly 2 sunny lines can cover all points. The solution claims impossible. Let's try to find a counterexample (i.e., a construction with k=2). If we can find one, the solution is wrong. If we can prove impossibility, solution is correct.\n\nWe need to consider the constraints on sunny lines: they cannot be vertical/horizontal/slope -1. So they can intersect each column at most one point. So each sunny line can cover at most one point per column. So across all columns, a sunny line can cover at most n points (one per column). But we need to cover all points. Let's count points per column:\n\n- Column 1: points (1,1) to (1,n). So n points.\n\n- Column 2: points (2,1) to (2,n-1). So n-1 points.\n\n- Column 3: points (3,1) to (3,n-2). So n-2 points.\n\n- ...\n\n- Column n-2: points (n-2,1) to (n-2,3). So 3 points.\n\n- Column n-1: points (n-1,1) to (n-1,2). So 2 points.\n\n- Column n: point (n,1). So 1 point.\n\nThus total points = n + (n-1) + (n-2) + ... + 3 + 2 + 1 = n(n+1)/2.\n\nNow, we have n-2 non-sunny lines. Non-sunny lines can be vertical, horizontal, or slope -1. Let's consider the maximum number of points a non-sunny line can cover.\n\n- A vertical line x = a covers n+1-a points (the entire column a). So vertical lines can cover many points.\n\n- A horizontal line y = b covers n+1-b points (the entire row b). So horizontal lines can also cover many points.\n\n- A slope -1 line x+y = c covers c-1 points (for c from 2 to n+1). So slope -1 lines can also cover many points.\n\nThus non-sunny lines can be used to cover many points, but we have only n-2 of them. So we need to allocate them wisely.\n\nWe need to see if we can cover all points with n-2 non-sunny lines and 2 sunny lines. Let's try to find a construction.\n\nOne approach: Use vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-2 (that's n-2 vertical lines). That covers all points in columns 1 to n-2. Then we have columns n-1 and n left uncovered. Column n-1 has points (n-1,1) and (n-1,2). Column n has point (n,1). We have 2 sunny lines left to cover these three points. Can we cover them with 2 sunny lines? Let's see.\n\nWe need to cover (n-1,1), (n-1,2), (n,1). A sunny line cannot be vertical/horizontal/slope -1. So we cannot use vertical line x=n-1 or x=n, nor horizontal y=1 or y=2, nor slope -1 line x+y = n (for (n-1,1) sum n) or x+y = n+1 (for (n-1,2) sum n+1) or x+y = n+1 (for (n,1) sum n+1). So we need to cover these points with two lines of other slopes.\n\nWe could try to use one sunny line to cover (n-1,1) and (n,1). But those two points have same y coordinate (y=1). So the line through them is horizontal y=1, which is not sunny. So we cannot use a sunny line to cover both (n-1,1) and (n,1). So we need separate lines for each point? Let's see.\n\nWe could try to use a sunny line to cover (n-1,2) and (n,1). The line through (n-1,2) and (n,1) has slope (1-2)/(n-(n-1)) = -1/1 = -1. That's slope -1, not sunny. So not allowed.\n\nWe could try to use a sunny line to cover (n-1,1) and (n-1,2). That's vertical line x=n-1, not sunny.\n\nThus any line that covers any two of these three points is either horizontal, vertical, or slope -1, all non-sunny. So we cannot cover any pair of these three points with a sunny line. So we need at least three lines (non-sunny) to cover them. But we have only 2 sunny lines left. So we cannot cover them with sunny lines. However, we could use non-sunny lines for some of them, but we have already used all n-2 non-sunny lines for columns 1 to n-2. But maybe we could allocate non-sunny lines differently: we could use some non-sunny lines to cover some of the points in columns n-1 and n, and use sunny lines for the rest. But we have only n-2 non-sunny lines total. Let's see if we can reduce the number of vertical lines used for columns 1 to n-2, perhaps using some horizontal or slope -1 lines to cover multiple columns simultaneously, freeing up some non-sunny lines for the rightmost col